# Pirate Battle — Architecture

## 1. Purpose

This document defines the initial architectural boundaries of Pirate Battle.

It describes how the main parts of the application interact and establishes constraints that implementations must preserve.

This document will evolve as the project is implemented.

Do not add speculative architecture that is not required by the challenge.

For global engineering principles, refer to `MASTER_SPEC.md`.

---

# 2. Architecture Overview

The application is divided into four primary areas:

```text
Application
│
├── React UI
│
├── Game
│
├── Data
│
└── Mock API
```

Each area owns a different type of state and responsibility.

Dependencies must remain explicit.

---

# 3. React UI

React owns application-level interface state.

Examples:

- current screen;
- menus;
- Options;
- Ranking;
- Match History;
- Result;
- dialogs;
- loading/error UI;
- accessibility structure.

React must not own continuous simulation state.

Examples of state that must not update through React every frame:

- ship position;
- ship rotation;
- projectile position;
- enemy position;
- collision state;
- per-frame cooldown progression.

---

# 4. Game Layer

The Game layer owns the active match.

Conceptually:

```text
Game
│
├── Core
├── Entities
├── Systems
├── Input
├── Rendering
├── Config
└── Utilities
```

The Game layer must be usable without depending on React rendering behavior.

React may create, destroy and observe a game instance, but must not drive the simulation frame-by-frame.

---

# 5. Game Core

The Core coordinates the simulation.

Expected responsibilities include:

```text
GameController
SimulationClock
GameState
GameConfigSnapshot
```

The controller orchestrates systems.

It should not become a large class containing every gameplay rule.

Gameplay logic should remain distributed across focused systems.

---

# 6. Entities

Entities represent objects participating in the simulation.

Expected entities include:

```text
Player
Enemy
├── Chaser
└── Shooter

Projectile
Island
```

Entities primarily contain identity and state.

Examples:

```text
position
rotation
health
velocity
status
```

Complex cross-entity behavior should remain in systems where practical.

---

# 7. Systems

Systems operate on game state.

Expected systems include:

```text
InputSystem
MovementSystem
CombatSystem
ProjectileSystem
CollisionSystem
EnemySystem
SpawnSystem
```

Systems should have focused responsibilities.

Avoid circular dependencies between systems.

When systems need shared state, that state should be owned by the game simulation rather than imported globally.

---

# 8. Simulation Flow

A simulation frame should conceptually follow a predictable sequence.

Example:

```text
Read Input
    ↓
Update Movement
    ↓
Update Enemy Behavior
    ↓
Update Combat
    ↓
Update Projectiles
    ↓
Resolve Collisions
    ↓
Process Spawning
    ↓
Update Match State
    ↓
Render Current State
```

The exact ordering may evolve when implementation proves another order is required.

Any change affecting gameplay correctness should be documented.

---

# 9. Simulation Time

Simulation correctness must not depend on rendering FPS.

All time-dependent systems use simulation time.

Conceptually:

```text
deltaTime
   ↓
Simulation
   ├── Movement
   ├── Projectiles
   ├── Cooldowns
   ├── AI
   ├── Spawning
   └── Match Timer
```

The simulation clock must support:

- pause;
- resume;
- deterministic testing;
- protection against unreasonable frame gaps.

---

# 10. Rendering

PixiJS renders the current game state.

Rendering is not the source of gameplay truth.

```text
Simulation State
      ↓
PixiJS Rendering
```

Avoid:

```text
Sprite Position
      ↓
Gameplay Logic
```

Gameplay systems should not depend on reading sprite state to determine simulation behavior.

The simulation owns position and rotation.

The renderer reflects them.

---

# 11. React ↔ Game Boundary

React and the game communicate through a small explicit interface.

Conceptually:

```text
React
  │
  │ create / pause / resume / destroy
  ↓
GameController

GameController
  │
  │ meaningful events / snapshots
  ↓
React
```

Possible events include:

```text
scoreChanged
playerHealthChanged
timeChanged
matchPaused
matchEnded
```

Do not expose every simulation update to React.

UI synchronization should happen only when necessary.

---

# 12. Input Architecture

Input follows:

```text
Keyboard ─┐
          ├──→ InputState ──→ Simulation
Touch ────┘
```

Browser event handlers update input state.

They must not directly:

- move ships;
- fire projectiles;
- damage entities;
- manipulate game rules.

This allows keyboard and touch to share the same gameplay implementation.

---

# 13. Collision Architecture

Collision belongs to the simulation.

Collision detection must not depend on DOM or React state.

Required relationships include:

```text
Player ↔ Arena
Player ↔ Island

Enemy ↔ Island

Player Projectile ↔ Enemy
Player Projectile ↔ Island

Enemy Projectile ↔ Player
Enemy Projectile ↔ Island

Chaser ↔ Player
```

Collision detection and collision consequences should remain separable where practical.

Example:

```text
Collision detected
       ↓
Combat / Game rule applied
```

This prevents collision code from becoming responsible for every gameplay rule.

---

# 14. Configuration

Gameplay balance is centralized.

Conceptually:

```text
GameConfig
│
├── session
├── player
├── weapons
├── projectiles
├── enemies
│   ├── chaser
│   └── shooter
└── spawn
```

Systems consume configuration.

Systems must not define their own unexplained balance constants.

At match start:

```text
Current Options
      +
Default Game Config
      ↓
Immutable Match Config Snapshot
      ↓
Game Instance
```

Changing Options during an active match must not modify that match.

---

# 15. Match Lifecycle

The Game instance owns one match.

```text
Created
   ↓
Loading
   ↓
Ready
   ↓
Running
   ↔
Paused
   ↓
Ended
   ↓
Destroyed
```

Restart creates a new clean match state.

Do not attempt to manually reset dozens of unrelated mutable objects when recreating the match is safer and simpler.

---

# 16. Resource Ownership

Every resource must have an identifiable owner.

Examples:

```text
Game instance
├── Pixi application
├── ticker callback
├── input listeners
├── visibility listener
├── resize listener
├── entities
├── containers
└── temporary effects
```

The owner that creates a resource is responsible for releasing it.

Destroying the game must leave no active gameplay:

- listeners;
- timers;
- loops;
- entities;
- effects.

This rule is especially important under React Strict Mode.

---

# 17. Data Architecture

Remote data follows:

```text
React Screen
     ↓
TanStack Query
     ↓
API Function
     ↓
Axios
     ↓
HTTP
     ↓
MSW
```

Do not call MSW handlers directly.

Do not bypass Axios for required Ranking or Match History requests.

Do not duplicate TanStack Query server state into application state without a concrete reason.

---

# 18. API Contracts

API contracts should live independently from UI components.

Conceptually:

```text
api/
├── contracts
├── client
├── ranking
└── match-history
```

MSW handlers consume the same contracts where practical.

This reduces differences between mocked API behavior and application expectations.

---

# 19. Persistence

Persistence must be isolated behind dedicated utilities or repositories.

Conceptually:

```text
Application
     ↓
Storage abstraction
     ↓
localStorage
```

Do not scatter direct `localStorage` calls across components and gameplay systems.

Persistence may include:

- Options;
- last completed match;
- pending submissions;
- mock API records.

Active simulation state is not persisted.

---

# 20. Match Submission

Completed match flow:

```text
Match Ends
    ↓
Result Created
    ↓
Persist Last Result
    ↓
Submit Match
    ↓
Success ─────→ Invalidate Relevant Queries
    │
    └ Failure → Persist Pending Submission
```

Submission must use a stable match identifier.

Retries use the same identifier.

The simulated backend must treat repeated submission of that identifier as the same match.

---

# 21. Dependency Direction

Prefer dependencies flowing toward domain behavior rather than UI details.

Conceptually:

```text
React UI
   ↓
Application / Game APIs
   ↓
Game Domain
```

The Game domain must not import React components.

Gameplay systems must not import UI screens.

API modules must not depend on visual components.

MSW handlers must not depend on React.

Avoid circular dependencies.

